              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Identified\" vulnerability refers to the use of certain C functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other security issues. In this case, the `printf` function is used in a way that could potentially lead to a format string vulnerability. \n\nA format string vulnerability occurs when the format string parameter (in this case, `argv[0]`) is not properly sanitized and can be controlled by an attacker. This can lead to a variety of attacks, including reading from or writing to arbitrary memory locations, or executing arbitrary code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to `printf` that do not have the same potential for format string vulnerabilities. For example, the `puts` or `fputs` functions can be used to print a string without interpreting format specifiers.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```cpp\nputs(argv[0]);\nputs(\"[--device=<device-id>] \");\nputs(\"[--i=<timing iterations> \");\nputs(\"[--n=<input items, default 40> \");\nputs(\"[--maxkey=<max key, default 20 (use -1 to test only unique keys)>]\");\nputs(\"[--v] \");\nputs(\"\\n\");\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library to execute properly:\n\n- `stdio.h` for `printf` function\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [CWE-134: Use of Externally-Controlled Format String](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/134.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "3717f050-7066-3d4b-bbbe-05f7ac18bc20",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues that can lead to arbitrary code execution or denial of service. In this case, the `printf` function is used, which can lead to format string vulnerabilities if the format string is not controlled by the programmer or if user-supplied data is used as a format string.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions, such as the `snprintf` function, which includes the size of the buffer as a parameter, preventing buffer overflows. Also, avoid using user-supplied data as a format string. Instead, use constant format strings and user-supplied data as arguments.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is the fixed version of the code:\n\n```cpp\nchar buffer[512];\nsnprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer),\n         \"Using device %d: %s ( SM%d, %d SMs, \"\n         \"%lld free / %lld total MB physmem, \"\n         \"%.3f GB/s @ %d kHz mem clock, ECC %s)\\n\",\n         dev,\n         deviceProp.name,\n         deviceProp.major * 100 + deviceProp.minor * 10,\n         deviceProp.multiProcessorCount,\n         (unsigned long long) device_free_physmem / 1024 / 1024,\n         (unsigned long long) device_total_physmem / 1024 / 1024,\n         device_giga_bandwidth,\n         deviceProp.memoryClockRate,\n         (deviceProp.ECCEnabled) ? \"on\" : \"off\");\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<stdio.h>` for the `snprintf` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "cad91462-e68d-3712-bd9a-5a49f5374c04",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions. The analysis detected that the source and destination may overlap based on data flow, which can cause undefined behavior when using memory copy operations like memcpy. Additionally, there is a risk that the source pointer may be null at this location, as there is no prior check ensuring it is non-null. The destination is likely safe from being null, as it is not a pointer-based member. The count argument is not directly derived from the destination's capacity, and there are no explicit guards or null-termination after the operation. These factors all contribute to the assessment that this is a genuine and actionable issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory, ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap before calling the memory copy function. If overlap is possible, use a function that safely handles overlapping regions, such as memmove. Additionally, check that the source pointer is not null before performing the copy to avoid null pointer dereference.\n\n**Remediation Example:**\n\n```cpp\nif (word_buff != nullptr && &key != word_buff) {\n    memcpy(&key, word_buff, sizeof(K));\n} else if (word_buff != nullptr) {\n    // If overlap is possible, use memmove instead\n    memmove(&key, word_buff, sizeof(K));\n}\n```\n\nThis approach first checks for null pointers and then ensures that if the source and destination overlap, memmove is used instead of memcpy. This prevents undefined behavior and potential security vulnerabilities.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            }
          ],
          "language": "en-US",
          "contents": [
            "localizedData",
            "nonLocalizedData"
          ],
          "isComprehensive": false
        }
